(  BIBLIOGRAPHY                                                     207) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CHAPTER I) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(INTRODUCTION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Thirty years have elapsed since the Jews began to enter the United) Tj T*
(States in numbers sufficiently large to make their immigration) Tj T*
(conspicuous in the general movement to this country. A study of Jewish) Tj T*
(immigration, in itself and in relation to the general movement,) Tj T*
(reveals an interesting phase of this historic and many-sided social) Tj T*
(phenomenon and throws light upon a number of important problems) Tj T*
(incident to it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Especially does it become clear that the Jewish immigration, although) Tj T*
(in part the result of the same forces as have affected the general) Tj T*
(immigration and the separate groups composing it, differs,) Tj T*
(nevertheless, in certain marked respects, from the typical) Tj T*
(immigration. Some of these differences indeed are fundamental and) Tj T*
(far-reaching in their effects and practically stamp the Jewish) Tj T*
(immigration as a movement _sui generis_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Generally speaking, in the forces which are behind the emigration of) Tj T*
(the Jews from the countries of the Old World, in the character of) Tj T*
(their immigration--its movement and its distinguishing qualities--the) Tj T*
(Jewish immigration strikes a distinctly individual note.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Three European countries--Russia, Austria-Hungary and) Tj T*
(Roumania--furnish the vast majority of the Jewish immigrants to the) Tj T*
(United States.[1] It is to these countries, therefore, that we must) Tj T*
(turn for light upon the causes of this movement.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
33 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 32 0 R >>
endobj
34 0 obj
<< /Length 3017 >>
stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
() Tj T*
(Geographically, these countries are closely connected; they form) Tj T*
(practically the whole of the division of Eastern Europe. Here the) Tj T*
(Slavonic races so largely predominate that the term Slavonic Europe) Tj T*
(has been applied to this section of Europe.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Eastern or Slavonic Europe is a social as well as a geographical fact.) Tj T*
(In racial stratification, economic and social institutions, cultural) Tj T*
(position and, in part, religious traditions as well, these countries) Tj T*
(present strong similarities to one another and equally strong) Tj T*
(differences in most of these respects from the countries of Western) Tj T*
(Europe.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is here that the Jews are found concentrated in the greatest) Tj T*
(numbers. Nearly seven and a half-million Jews--more than half of the) Tj T*
(Jews of the world--live in these countries. Of this number more than) Tj T*
(five millions are in Russia, more than two millions in Austria-Hungary,) Tj T*
(and a quarter of a million in Roumania. The great majority of these are) Tj T*
(massed on the contiguous borders, in a zone which embraces Poland, and) Tj T*
(Western Russia, Galicia, and Moldavia. This is the emigration zone. The) Tj T*
(relative density of the Jews is greatest in these parts. Every seventh) Tj T*
(man in Poland, every ninth man in Western Russia and in Galicia, and) Tj T*
(every tenth man in Moldavia, is a Jew. Thus the center of gravity of) Tj T*
(the Jewish populations is still the former kingdom of Poland, as it was) Tj T*
(constituted before the partitions at the end of the eighteenth century.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(United originally in Poland, the Jews of Eastern Europe still retain) Tj T*
(the same general characteristics, in spite of the changes that have) Tj T*
(been brought about by a century of rule under different governments.) Tj T*
(Speaking a common language, Yiddish, and possessing common religious) Tj T*
(traditions, as well as similar social and psychological traits, the) Tj T*
(East-European Jews present on the whole a striking uniformity of) Tj T*
(character.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Through the centuries they have become deeply rooted in the) Tj T*
(East-European soil, their economic and social life intimately) Tj T*
(connected with the economic and social conditions of these countries) Tj T*
(and their history deeply influenced by the transformations that have) Tj T*
(been taking place in them for half a century.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As these conditions and transformations furnish the foundation of) Tj T*
(Jewish life in Eastern Europe, and contain the explanation of the) Tj T*
(situation that has been largely responsible for the recent Jewish) Tj T*
(emigration to Western Europe and the United States, a rapid review of) Tj T*
(the economic, social and political conditions of Russia, Roumania and) Tj T*
(Austria-Hungary will be made.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(FOOTNOTES:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([1] _Cf. infra_, p. 95.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
