36 0 obj
<< /Length 2678 >>
stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(PART I) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE CAUSES OF JEWISH EMIGRATION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CHAPTER II) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(EASTERN EUROPE: ECONOMIC, SOCIAL AND POLITICAL CONDITIONS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I. RUSSIA) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The difficulty of the average American to understand the character of) Tj T*
(Russian life, some traits of which have been so vividly brought home) Tj T*
(to him in recent years, may be attributed to a general idea that a) Tj T*
(country rubbing elbows as it were with Western civilization for) Tj T*
(several centuries must perforce itself possess the characteristics of) Tj T*
(modern civilization. A closer survey of the economic, social and) Tj T*
(political conditions prevailing in Russia to-day, however, reveals) Tj T*
(many points of difference from those of the countries of Western) Tj T*
(Europe, and presents a remarkable contrast with those prevailing in) Tj T*
(the United States. Russia and the United States, indeed, stand, in) Tj T*
(Leroy-Beaulieu's phrase, at the two poles of modern civilization. So) Tj T*
(far apart are they in the character of their economic, social and) Tj T*
(political structures, in the degree in which they utilize the forms) Tj T*
(and institutions of modern life, and, in the difference in the mental) Tj T*
(make-up of their peoples, that there exist few, if any, points of real) Tj T*
(contact.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Up to the middle of the 19th century, Russia was, in nearly all) Tj T*
(respects, a medieval state. She was a society, which, in the words of) Tj T*
(Kovalevsky, "preserved still of feudalism, not its political spirit) Tj T*
(but its economic structure, serfdom, monopoly and the privileges of) Tj T*
(the nobility, its immunities in the matter of taxes, its exclusive) Tj T*
(right to landed property, and its seignorial rights."[2] Her modern) Tj T*
(era dates from the emancipation of the serfs in 1861, when she became,) Tj T*
(at least in form, a European state. But, though the Russia of our day) Tj T*
(has witnessed great transformations in the direction of modernization,) Tj T*
(she still retains many of the conditions and much of the spirit of her) Tj T*
(medieval past.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A rapid review of the economic, social and political conditions of) Tj T*
(Russia will serve to make clearer this situation, which has an) Tj T*
(important bearing upon the exceptional position, legal, economic,) Tj T*
(social, of the Jews in the Empire, and upon the fateful events of) Tj T*
(their history for a third of a century.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The most striking fact in the economic life of present-day Russia is) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
37 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 36 0 R >>
endobj
38 0 obj
<< /Length 3441 >>
stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(that it is overwhelmingly agricultural. More than three-fourths of her) Tj T*
(population are engaged in some form of agricultural labor. The vast) Tj T*
(majority are peasants living in villages. Towns are relatively few and) Tj T*
(sparsely populated. Agricultural products constitute 85 per cent of) Tj T*
(the annual exports. What a contrast does this agricultural state, this) Tj T*
("peasant empire", present to the industrially and commercially) Tj T*
(developed countries of Western Europe and the United States!) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Russian peasant still practices a primitive system of agriculture.) Tj T*
(His method of extensive cultivation, the three-field system in vogue,) Tj T*
(his primitive implements, his domestic economy of half a century ago,) Tj T*
(with its home production for home consumption, which is still) Tj T*
(maintained in many parts of Russia to this day--all these present) Tj T*
(conditions not far removed from those of the middle ages of Western) Tj T*
(Europe.[3]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The existence to our day of this almost primitive economy finds its) Tj T*
(explanation in the fact that serfdom existed in Russia, in all its) Tj T*
(unmitigated cruelty, until comparatively recent times. Its abolition) Tj T*
(through the Emancipation Act of Alexander II--antedating our own) Tj T*
(Emancipation Proclamation by a few years--struck off the chains that) Tj T*
(bound twenty millions of peasants to the soil. The emancipation,) Tj T*
(however, was not complete. The land the peasants received was) Tj T*
(insufficient for their needs. Other conditions co-operated in the) Tj T*
(course of time with this primary one, to create a situation of chronic) Tj T*
(starvation for the great mass of the Russian peasants. Forced by the) Tj T*
