(II. ROUMANIA) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Up to very recent years, the history of the Jews in Roumania centers) Tj T*
(about those resident in Moldavia. Its proximity to ancient Poland and) Tj T*
(close association with Bessarabia, naturally made for a back-and-forth) Tj T*
(movement of the Polish and Russian Jews, whose settlement was invited) Tj T*
(by the boyars or landed nobility because of resulting industrial and) Tj T*
(commercial advantages.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The position of the Jews in Moldavia up to the middle of the) Tj T*
(nineteenth century did not differ to any extent from that of their) Tj T*
(brethren in Russia. Moldavia, as a Christian state, denied civil and) Tj T*
(political rights to all non-Christians. The Jews in Moldavia were) Tj T*
(regarded as aliens, whose activities were subject to special) Tj T*
(regulation. The beginning of the last century witnessed the first) Tj T*
(special Jewish laws. The Jews were forbidden to buy the products of) Tj T*
(the soil, to acquire real property; non-resident Jews were debarred) Tj T*
(unless they could prove an occupation and show the possession of) Tj T*
(property. Definite restrictions as to occupation, residence in the) Tj T*
(villages, the ownership, in villages, of houses, land, vineyards,) Tj T*
(_etc._, existed. As vagabonds they could be expelled from the country) Tj T*
(by administrative decree. Thus was their legal status fixed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The emancipation of Jews was first demanded by the liberal party) Tj T*
(during the revolutionary days of 1848. But no practical change) Tj T*
(resulted until the Convention of Paris in 1856, which, in granting) Tj T*
(autonomy to the two provinces, guaranteed civil rights to all) Tj T*
(Moldavians, regardless of creed. Though political rights were granted) Tj T*
(only to Christian Moldo-Wallachians, the provision was made that, by) Tj T*
(legislative arrangements, the enjoyment of political rights could be) Tj T*
(extended to other creeds. Thus was established the possibility of a) Tj T*
(gradual emancipation of the Jews, foreshadowed in the communal law of) Tj T*
(1864, which granted the right of naturalization to certain classes of) Tj T*
(native Roumanian Jews. Those who had passed through college or had a) Tj T*
(recognized foreign degree, or who had founded a factory in the land) Tj T*
(employing at least fifty workmen were among the favored classes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Shortly afterwards, this section was abrogated, and, with the) Tj T*
(abdication of the liberal Couza and the accession of Charles) Tj T*
(Hohenzollern, the present king, to the throne, the situation changed.) Tj T*
(Article VII of the constitution of the newly-created kingdom read that) Tj T*
(foreigners not of the Christian faith could not be naturalized. As) Tj T*
(within the term foreigner the great mass of the Jews residing in the) Tj T*
(land was included, this was a denial of the conditions laid down in) Tj T*
(the Treaty of Paris. At the same time, old laws against the Jews which) Tj T*
(had fallen into abeyance were revived, expulsions of the Jews from the) Tj T*
(villages into the towns began to take place with great frequency, laws) Tj T*
(requiring all sellers of liquor in rural communes to be naturalized) Tj T*
(Roumanians deprived many Jewish families of a livelihood--in short,) Tj T*
(the usual symptoms of anti-Jewish activity became the order of the) Tj T*
(day.) Tj T*
