(and in the last decade, numbering 47,301 and constituting 4.8 per cent) Tj T*
(of the total immigration of the decade.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Jewish immigration from Austria-Hungary bore a proportion to the) Tj T*
(total higher in the first two decades, contributing 44,619 immigrants) Tj T*
(in the first decade and 83,720 immigrants in the second decade, or) Tj T*
(23.1 per cent and 21.3 per cent, respectively, of the total, and) Tj T*
(152,810 immigrants, or 15.7 per cent, in the last decade.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Jewish immigrants from the United Kingdom and British North) Tj T*
(America, which, in the first two decades constituting less than one) Tj T*
(per cent of the total of each decade, were included in the rubric "all) Tj T*
(others", rose in the last decade to 42,589, constituting 4.4 per cent,) Tj T*
(and to 9,701, constituting one per cent, of the total of this decade.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An examination of the yearly contributions made by the Jews of the) Tj T*
(principal countries[62] shows that the immigrants from Russia formed) Tj T*
(the majority of the immigrants for each year of the entire period, and) Tj T*
(as a rule, did not deviate far from the general proportion established) Tj T*
(for the thirty years. The greatest increases occurred during the years) Tj T*
(of maximum Jewish immigration, in 1882, 1891, 1892 and 1906, when the) Tj T*
(Russian Jewish immigrants constituted four-fifths or more of the total) Tj T*
(for the year.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
