() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The movement of the total Jewish immigration for the thirty years) Tj T*
(becomes clear in the light of the preceding pages. It is a rising) Tj T*
(movement, divided into two parts, the first culminating in 1892 and the) Tj T*
(second culminating in 1906. Like the Russian Jewish immigration which) Tj T*
(underlies it, the movement is one of geometrical progression.[86] From) Tj T*
(1881 to 1890, 193,021 Jewish immigrants entered this country, 12.3 per) Tj T*
(cent of the total Jewish immigration. From 1891 to 1900, 393,516 Jewish) Tj T*
(immigrants, or 25.2 per cent entered. In the last decade there entered) Tj T*
(the enormous number of 976,263 Jewish immigrants, representing 62.5 per) Tj T*
(cent of the total Jewish immigration for the thirty years. This was) Tj T*
(more than twice as many as had entered the preceding decade, and more) Tj T*
(than five times the number of those who had entered the first decade.) Tj T*
(The Jewish immigration is in its largest part a product of the last) Tj T*
(decade.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The rise has not, however, been uniformly steady, as a division of the) Tj T*
(entire period into five six-year periods shows.[87] In the period from) Tj T*
(1893 to 1898, there was a fall in the Jewish immigration. This period) Tj T*
(coincides with the years of depression in the United States following) Tj T*
(the panic of 1893. The fall was chiefly due to that in the Russian) Tj T*
(Jewish immigration. The Jewish immigration from Austria-Hungary on the) Tj T*
(contrary showed a relative rise. For this period, as well as for a) Tj T*
(few years before, the Roumanian Jewish immigration contributed smaller) Tj T*
(numbers than in the previous decade. As in the case of the Russian) Tj T*
(Jewish movement, if we compare the immigration of the six-year period) Tj T*
(from 1885 to 1890, with that from 1893 to 1898, omitting the years) Tj T*
(1891 and 1892 which are influenced in their great rise by the) Tj T*
(exceptional circumstances occurring within these two years, we find) Tj T*
(that the Jewish immigration was higher during the latter period of) Tj T*
(depression in the United States than during the earlier period, the) Tj T*
(total number of immigrants being 167,567 for the latter period, and) Tj T*
(153,951 for the former.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the period from 1899 to 1904 there was a great rise. A quarter of) Tj T*
(the entire immigration came in this period. The largest number of) Tj T*
(immigrants--more than two-fifths of the total of thirty years--came in) Tj T*
(the period from 1905 to 1910. If we included the immigration of 1904,) Tj T*
(which properly belongs to the later movement, we find that half of the) Tj T*
(entire Jewish immigration came within the seven years from 1904 to) Tj T*
(1910.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The yearly variations of the total Jewish immigration correspond) Tj T*
(closely in the main to those of the Russian Jewish movement.[88] The) Tj T*
(influence of the other movements is, however, felt, at times quite) Tj T*
(strongly. Before 1885 the total Jewish immigration was quite small;) Tj T*
(less than 10,000 \(except in 1882\) or less than 1 per cent of the) Tj T*
(total, arrived each year. The rise of the immigration in 1882 to) Tj T*
(13,202 was wholly due to the increase in the number of Russian Jewish) Tj T*
(immigrants. The second half of this decade was marked by a rising tide) Tj T*
