(the great proportion of females and children found in it.[94] From) Tj T*
(1899 to 1910, out of a total immigration of 1,074,442 Jews, 607,822,) Tj T*
(or 56.6 per cent were males, and 466,620, or 43.4 per cent, were) Tj T*
(females. These proportions have varied but slightly throughout the) Tj T*
(period. The greatest departures were in the years 1904 and 1905. The) Tj T*
(increase of the immigration of males in these years is explained by) Tj T*
(the unusual conditions existing in Russia at this time--economic) Tj T*
(unrest, revolution--which had the effect of sending over the men as an) Tj T*
(_avantgarde_ to prepare the way for their families. Young men fleeing) Tj T*
(to escape conscription also swelled the numbers. In 1906, however, the) Tj T*
(number of males decreased by 2,000, but that of females increased by) Tj T*
(more than 25,000. In this tremendous increase of females is registered) Tj T*
