(States. About an equal proportion of each was destined for the South) Tj T*
(Atlantic States. A much smaller proportion of the Jewish than of the) Tj T*
(total was destined for the Western States. In view of the industrial) Tj T*
(equipment of the Jewish immigrants discussed previously, this tendency) Tj T*
(is explained.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Jewish immigrants destined for the eastern states play a) Tj T*
(correspondingly large part among the total number destined for these) Tj T*
(states.[138] The Jewish immigrants destined for the North Atlantic) Tj T*
(States were 14.5 per cent of all the immigrants destined for this) Tj T*
(division. Their next highest proportion was of those destined for the) Tj T*
(South Central States, of which they constituted 9.9 per cent. They) Tj T*
(constituted an almost equal proportion of the immigrants destined for) Tj T*
(the North Central and the South Central States, 5.2 per cent, and 5.0) Tj T*
(per cent, respectively. Of the immigrants destined for the Western) Tj T*
(States they constituted only 1.2 per cent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The final destination of the immigrants very frequently is different) Tj T*
(from the destination stated at the time of landing. An examination of) Tj T*
(the disposition of Jewish immigrants landing at the port of New York) Tj T*
(from 1886 to 1906 showed that a large part of the immigrants left) Tj T*
(within a very short time for other parts.[139] Of the 918,388) Tj T*
(immigrants that landed at the port of New York, from 1886 to 1906,) Tj T*
(669,453, or 72.9 per cent, remained in New York, and 248,935, or 27.1) Tj T*
(per cent, left for other points.) Tj T*
