(CHAPTER VI) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(SUMMARY AND CONCLUSIONS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Some of the principal characteristics of the Jewish immigration to the) Tj T*
(United States have been presented in the preceding pages. The Jewish) Tj T*
(immigration has been shown to consist essentially of permanent) Tj T*
(settlers. Its family movement is incomparable in degree, and contains) Tj T*
(a larger relative proportion as well as absolute number of women and) Tj T*
(children, than any other immigrant people. This in turn is reflected) Tj T*
(in the greater relative proportion as well as absolute number of those) Tj T*
(classified as having "no occupation". The element of dependency thus) Tj T*
(predicated is another indication of the family composition of the) Tj T*
(Jewish immigration. Its return movement is the smallest of any, as) Tj T*
(compared both with its large immigration and the number of total) Tj T*
(emigrants. The Jewish immigrants are distinguished as well by a larger) Tj T*
(relative proportion and absolute number of skilled laborers, than any) Tj T*
(other immigrant people. In these four primary characteristics the) Tj T*
(Jewish immigrants stand apart from all the others.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is with the neighboring Slavic races emigrating from the countries) Tj T*
(of Eastern Europe and with whom the Jewish immigrants are closely) Tj T*
(associated that the contrasts, in all these respects, are strongest.) Tj T*
(The Slavic immigrants are chiefly male adults. Their movement is) Tj T*
(largely composed of transients, as evidenced by a relatively large) Tj T*
(outward movement and emphasized by the fact that the vast majority of) Tj T*
(them are unskilled laborers. An exception, in large measure, must be) Tj T*
(made of the Bohemian and Moravian immigrants who present) Tj T*
(characteristics strongly similar to those of the Jewish immigrants.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The division into "old" and "new" immigration brings out even more) Tj T*
(clearly the exceptional position of the Jews in regard to these) Tj T*
(characteristics. Although the Jewish immigration has been) Tj T*
(contemporaneous with the "new" immigration from Eastern and) Tj T*
(Southeastern Europe, and is furthermore essentially East-European in) Tj T*
(origin, its characteristics place it altogether with the "old") Tj T*
(immigration.[140] Most striking, however is the fact that in all of) Tj T*
ET
